WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.363
Please welcome Emma Corrin and Josh O'Connor.

2
00:00:04.363 --> 00:00:07.363
(audience cheering)

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.160
I love seeing how different you are in real life.

4
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.103
Isn't hair, makeup, wardrobe, just everything?

5
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.490
It's the best, honestly.
It is.

6
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.580
I mean, it's so exciting for us to see you guys like this

7
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.080
you know, and when I'm doing my thing,

8
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.620
it's like, I can see what you really look like.

9
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.320
And then I have to say also,

10
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.243
I'm immediately transported the second I'm in.

11
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.240
And I too, as an actor, it's like it changes

12
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.310
your body language when you put on the clothes.

13
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.510
And everybody's marveling at these side-by-sides

14
00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.030
of how impeccably right that you've got it.

15
00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.220
And it does make a huge difference

16
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.489
because then everybody can sort of fall

17
00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:56.130
into the history of...

18
00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.410
I mean, I really don't know how else to explain it

19
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.370
other than, this was at the center of all of our universes.

20
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.928
And I do want to share something with you.

21
00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:09.330
Okay, we probably have a screenshot of it.

22
00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.590
But this is actually here,

23
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.400
I keep this with me at the studio.

24
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.046
There is me presenting the ET doll to Princess Diana

25
00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:20.070
when I was seven years old, in 1982,

26
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.520
at the World Premier of "ET".

27
00:01:22.468 --> 00:01:23.720
Oh. That's...

28
00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.240
That's very sweet.

29
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.700
And it was a moment that I will never forget.

30
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.400
And I keep the picture nearby,

31
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.400
because I really was the girl amongst millions

32
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.550
of other girls who grew up with Princess Diana.

33
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:44.550
And I mean, Emma, what was it like to get into her?

34
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.570
You're so amazing as her.

35
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.090
And I just want to say,

36
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.780
that the first scene you guys have together, to me,

37
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.320
with so poetically choreographed.

38
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.770
And with all the history that we know

39
00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.150
about Charles and Diana,

40
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.933
to set the stage in this very coquettish, eyeball,

41
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.770
mysterious, Shakespearian way was really amazing.

42
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.773
Did you like the introduction of these characters?

43
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.250
Yeah, I really did.

44
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.960 line:15%
I think you've kind of hit the nail on the head there

45
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.450 line:15%
by saying it sort of Shakespearian.

46
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.460 line:15%
Because there's such a historical romance

47
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.920 line:15%
I suppose, associated with that kind of depiction

48
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.570
of two characters meeting

49
00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.420
that almost transcends all kind of reality.

50
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.810
And I guess it kind of goes hand-in-hand

51
00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.710
with the whole fairy tale thing

52
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.080
that they have going on at the beginning of the series.

53
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.550
I thought it was a really beautiful way

54
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.730
of doing that introduction.

55
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.140
Yeah, I agree.

56
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.640
Yeah, we don't (indistinct) too much, I suppose.

57
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.500
It's like we wanted more going into it,

58
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.100
and then that scene set us up

59
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.910
for just wanting more and more and more.

60
00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.290
Josh, I must say, that your acting

61
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.880
with your eyes and your body language,

62
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.540
I also noticed that.

63
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.020
First of all, when actors do internal, I'm sorry,

64
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.490
but it's usually snoozy and boring.

65
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.710
I'm like, I hope you're having fun

66
00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.390
because I'm not, watching it because you're internal

67
00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.400
and I need something.

68
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.440
You do internal so well.

69
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.980
I was so gripped with your eyes and your shoulders.

70
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.940
You could not say anything in an entire scene

71
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.410
and I was riveted.

72
00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.860
And I also loved how I felt like, as a viewer,

73
00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.640
I thought I saw your body language

74
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.580
really change when you were with Emma.

75
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.420
Like you were more confident, and more of a cad.

76
00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.320
And like your demeanor changed.

77
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.950
Was that conscientious?

78
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.150
'Cause I totally picked that up as a viewer.

79
00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.003
Thank you.

80
00:03:44.003 --> 00:03:47.250 line:15%
Yeah, I suppose, we did lots of work on the kind

81
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.640 line:15%
of physicality of these characters.

82
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.640 line:15%
I think you can tell so much with sort of body language,

83
00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.430 line:15%
as you know.

84
00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.490
And I think particularly with Charles,

85
00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.410
there's kind of very early on

86
00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.120
where you sort of picked up on this sort of stoop

87
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.840
he has, and the way he kind of leans his neck out.

88
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.450
And we worked with Polly Bennett,

89
00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:09.690
who's a brilliant movement coach.

90
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.360
And we, we kind of, both Emma and I were keen

91
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.680
to find sort of why they stand like that.

92
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.630
Or where that body language is rooted from.

93
00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.510
And so, as we went through the series, we had this sort

94
00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.090
of like ongoing joke about just how far Charles does stoop.

95
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.320
And that, as the pressure of the world gets more,

96
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.260
he just sort of winds up like a hunchback really.

97
00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.403
I mean, it's quite, it was quite intense.

98
00:04:33.403 --> 00:04:36.520
You, in your own life, I heard a story

99
00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.720
about a little date where you showed up,

100
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.143
that wasn't so smooth.

101
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.160
Yeah. I mean, this is quite a long time ago, Drew.

102
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.940
So let's just start with that.

103
00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.250
Oh, way in the rear-view mirror.

104
00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:50.550
Yeah. Just like I said, this isn't a recent occurrence.

105
00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:55.550
But yeah, I was maybe like 13, 14

106
00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:58.830
and I did take a girl, it was on a double date.

107
00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.530
And my mum had given me not very much money.

108
00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.710
So I only had enough money to take us to the cinema.

109
00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.580
And we went to the cinema and then it was Easter.

110
00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.010
And afterwards, the other guy was like

111
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.960
let's go and get these girls some eggs, like chocolate eggs.

112
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.700
And we went to the chocolate shop

113
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.410
and he got this beautiful egg with the girl's name,

114
00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.230
sort of printed with beautiful chocolate writing.

115
00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.997
And I got a small egg and then realized, I had no money.

116
00:05:28.997 --> 00:05:30.490
And so she had to buy her own,

117
00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.550
which was less than brilliant.

118
00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.030
You could just tell her, "Listen I'm going

119
00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:37.620
to be playing Prince Charles in my future.

120
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.357
So, don't worry about"
I should have done.

121
00:05:39.357 --> 00:05:41.527
"The size of the egg."

122
00:05:41.527 --> 00:05:44.850
Emma, I loved a detail, before we go,

123
00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.980
that you said, when...

124
00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.810
I don't know who you were saying this with,

125
00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.970
but that when you felt the nerves of all the attention

126
00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.630
being Diana, and all of that energy,

127
00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.770
that you should channel it into the performance.

128
00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.430
Who do have that conversation with?

129
00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:02.640
So that's a conversation I had with Ben Caron,

130
00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.696
one of the directors, just after I got the part actually.

131
00:06:06.696 --> 00:06:08.340 line:15%
And Ben's sitting me down and saying,

132
00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.800 line:15%
you know, life's going to change a lot.

133
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.070 line:15%
And, any time you feel overwhelmed or nervous,

134
00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.300
or there are people taking photographs of you,

135
00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.570
or you end up in the news, use it.

136
00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.700
Because it's a completely parallel experience

137
00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.110
that you're having.

138
00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.240
And it really did come in handy.

139
00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.550
Yeah, I remember one day we were filming in London

140
00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.940
on the street, and the scene was me being chased

141
00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.350
by paparazzi as young Diana.

142
00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.930
And then, behind the supporting actors,

143
00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.760
who were playing the paparazzi were actual paparazzi

144
00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.320
who were there for me.

145
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.410
And it was such a weird, like yeah, colliding of worlds.

146
00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.040
But actually weirdly useful.

147
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.470
Wow. I think that's a very smart direction

148
00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:54.760
and you took it so well.

149
00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.990
And you're honoring these people who we've grown up with

150
00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.930
that are such icons in this world

151
00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.490
and doing them such justice.

152
00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.630
And we love it.

153
00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.110
And I think you guys are incredible.

154
00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:08.070
And I was so thrilled that you would come on the show today.

155
00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:09.340
Thank you, Emma and Josh.

156
00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.540
Season four of "The Crown" is out now.

157
00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.730
Everybody knows it. Everybody's watching it.

158
00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.100
It's the talk of everything.

159
00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.150
Thank you.

160
00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.733
(upbeat music)

